  "source": "FR:2765-CCAF",
  "fluff": {
    "overview": "<p>The Black Lion I-class battlecruiser was the most formidable WarShip of the early spacefaring age when it entered service in 2315, commissioned by Boeing Interstellar at its Terra orbital facilities. The class was purpose-built to serve as a fleet flagship, designed to project overwhelming force during the founding of the Terran Hegemony and the violent competition among humanity's emerging great powers. The Black Lion I carried the heaviest armament of any vessel in its generation, and its sheer scale made it the most feared combatant of the Age of War. Though relatively few were built compared to later Star League designs, the class earned a fearsome reputation that few contemporary vessels could challenge, and Boeing Interstellar's success with the design established the company as a premier builder of capital combatants.</p>",
    "capabilities": "<p>At 720,000 tons and 743 meters in length, the Black Lion I was among the largest WarShips constructed during the Age of War. The primary armament consisted of thirty-two NAC/20 naval autocannons distributed across all firing arcs, delivering catastrophic kinetic damage at the close ranges that Age of War engagements typically demanded. Secondary batteries of sixty-four AC/5 and sixty-four AC/2 conventional autocannons provided anti-fighter and point-defense coverage, creating overlapping fields of fire that made approaching the vessel extremely hazardous for small craft and assault DropShips. Twelve Killer Whale capital missile launchers provided devastating long-range striking power, allowing engagement of enemy capital ships well before entering autocannon range.</p><p>The 1,954 single heat sinks reflected the pre-double heat sink technology of the era, requiring careful fire discipline during sustained engagements despite the massive heat sink count. Standard armor protection totaling 1,080 tons provided excellent resilience, while a safe thrust of 1.5 gravities with a 2.5-gravity emergency rating offered adequate maneuverability for a vessel of its mass. The crew complement of over 700 personnel included 140 marines for boarding actions and 70 passenger berths, reflecting the flagship role the class was designed to fulfill. Twenty-four small craft bays supported reconnaissance and shuttle operations, though the class carried no docking collars for DropShip transport.</p>",
    "deployment": "<p>James McKenna flew his flag from a Black Lion I during the key campaigns that established the Terran Hegemony as the dominant power in human-settled space, and the class became synonymous with the Hegemony's naval might during the violent Age of War. Black Lion Is served as the heavy striking arm of the Hegemony fleet, their broadsides proving decisive in fleet engagements against the nascent navies of the Inner Sphere's emerging Great Houses. The vessels typically anchored battle squadrons, screening lighter combatants while closing to effective range where their devastating batteries could be brought to bear.</p><p>No Black Lion Is are known to have survived into the Succession Wars era. Their age and the technological limitations inherent in their pre-Star League construction rendered them impractical to maintain as newer designs became available, and the class passed from active service without fanfare as the navy that had built them evolved beyond the need for such vessels.</p>",
    "history": "<p>The Black Lion I-class battlecruiser emerged from Boeing Interstellar's Terra orbital shipyards in 2315 as the most powerful WarShip humanity had yet produced. The twenty-fourth century was a period of explosive military expansion as the great powers of human-settled space competed for dominance, and McKenna understood that control of the space lanes between star systems was the key to projecting power across interstellar distances. The Black Lion I was his answer, a battlecruiser that combined the firepower of a battleship with sufficient speed to operate independently or as the centerpiece of a fast-moving battle squadron.</p><p>Boeing Interstellar designed the class to share structural components with the Dreadnought-class battleship then under concurrent development, an efficient production strategy that allowed the Terra shipyards to build both classes from common hull sections, drive assemblies, and armor plate. This approach enabled the Hegemony to field a credible capital ship fleet far more quickly than would otherwise have been possible, and the Black Lion I became the symbol of Hegemony naval power throughout the Age of War. The sight of one emerging from a jump point was often sufficient to end a confrontation before it began, as few powers of the era possessed anything capable of standing against its broadside batteries in a fleet engagement.</p><p>McKenna himself flew his flag from a Black Lion I during the campaigns that consolidated Terran control over the inner colonies, and the class served as the heavy striking arm of the Hegemony fleet for decades. The sheer scale of the vessel and its devastating firepower made it the most feared WarShip of its generation, and the design influenced Hegemony naval doctrine for more than a century after its introduction. As WarShip technology matured through the twenty-fourth and twenty-fifth centuries, however, the Black Lion I's limitations became increasingly apparent. Its single heat sink technology, enormous by contemporary standards but inadequate by later ones, constrained sustained fire rates during prolonged engagements. The safe thrust rating fell below the performance expected of newer designs, and the class predated many of the technological advances that would define Star League-era WarShip construction, including double heat sinks, ferro-carbide armor, and compact KF drives.</p><p>The Star League Navy ultimately retired the Black Lion I from frontline service, replacing it with the Aegis-class heavy cruiser, a design that incorporated lessons learned from decades of operating the older battlecruiser into a more modern and capable hull. Boeing Interstellar would later revisit the Black Lion lineage in 2691 with the Black Lion II-class battlecruiser, a thoroughly modernized design that retained the name and general concept while incorporating Star League-era technology throughout. The original Black Lion I's legacy, however, lies not in its descendants but in its role as the WarShip that established Terran naval supremacy during humanity's most formative and violent period of interstellar expansion.</p>",
    "manufacturer": "Boeing Interstellar",
    "primaryFactory": "Terra"
  }
}